{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T5HFc5WRe9Cq1PnbHTw3mL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Let me analyze the project context carefully and propose tasks.\n\n## Step 1: Analyze Project Context\n\nThis is a Go project that re-implements Unix utilities from specifications derived from C source code. The pipeline is: extract specs from C → write PRDs → generate Go code → verify with differential tests against GNU reference binaries.\n\n## Step 2: Summarize Project State\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specifications. Re-implementations diverge from reference behavior on edge cases.\n\n**Architecture**:\n- `cmd/` - per-utility Go implementations\n- `pkg/testutils/` - differential testing harness\n- `pkg/sys/` - platform syscall abstractions\n\n\n- `pkg/format/` - output formatting (colors, columns, sizes)\n- `magefiles/` - build targets\n- `docs/` - specs, PRDs, use cases\n\nLooking at what's already implemented, I can see several utilities have working code: `cat`, `du`, `sponge`, and `version` all have main implementations, with test files for most of them.\n\nBeyond those, I need to build out the `wc` command along with its tests, then tackle the formatting package with color, column, and size handling. There's also platform-specific system utilities for file info on Darwin and Linux, plus terminal handling and test utilities to implement.\n\nLooking at the release roadmap, I've got four batches to work through: the foundational infrastructure in 00.0 with magefiles, format, sys, and testutils packages all at spec complete but not yet started, then the wc implementation in 01.0, followed by subsequent batches in 01.1 and beyond.\n\nBut I'm noticing a discrepancy—the code_status field marks everything as \"not started,\" yet the source_code field shows these packages already have implementations. Since the source code is the authoritative reference, it looks like the status metadata is outdated and all these releases actually have their code in place.\n\nLooking more carefully at what's actually in the source_code, I can see that `cmd/du/main.go` exists but there's no corresponding test file listed, whereas the other command packages all have their tests included. So the missing piece appears to be the test coverage for the du command. I need to examine what PRD requirements the du tests should cover and follow the same differential testing pattern used in the other utility tests, checking which requirements are actually implemented in the du main.go file.\n\nLooking at the output formatting flags like -d for summarize mode, -k for kilobytes, -m for megabytes, -c for grand total, and --apparent-size, plus the hard-link deduplication logic and error handling with SIGPIPE support.\n\nFor the test design, I'm following the established pattern where TestMain builds the Go binary and locates the reference binary (gdu), then individual test functions run differential tests comparing outputs. Given the constraints of one task maximum, four requirements, 250 lines, and 5-7 files, I need to scope this carefully.\n\nI'm planning to create the test file following the same structure as the existing wc and cat tests, which means I'll need to read the du implementation, the testutils harness, and example test patterns to understand how to properly set up the differential tests. The PRD will also be essential for understanding the expected behaviors to test. I'm mapping out the specific flags and behaviors that need test coverage, then narrowing down to four key requirements: setting up the test harness with the Go binary and reference implementation, validating the core traversal and reporting functionality, and testing how different flags interact with each other.\n\nLooking at the test file sizes from similar tools, I need to keep this under 250 lines, so I'm focusing on the most critical differential behaviors rather than trying to cover every flag combination exhaustively. Looking at the PRD requirements for du, I need to map out which ones matter most for testing. The core functionality breaks down into traversal and size calculation, output formatting with various flags, hard-link deduplication, and error handling. With the constraint of 4 requirements per task, I'll anchor my test structure to the test infrastructure setup, then cover the essential traversal and reporting behaviors, and finally the most commonly used flag combinations.\n\nFor implementation, I'm following the established patterns from the wc and cat test suites, using the same test harness approach with a reference binary comparison strategy. I'll set up temporary directories with known file structures to ensure the size calculations are deterministic and comparable against GNU du.\n\nSince this is a test-only task, creating just the one test file is appropriate—the constraints about file count are guidelines for production code, not hard requirements for test coverage.\n\nNow I need to identify the reference materials: the du implementation itself in main.go, the differential test utilities in testutils, and existing test patterns from wc and cat to follow. I should also pull in the PRD for du to understand the requirements being tested, plus the relevant test suite specification.\n\nLooking at the naming convention from other tools, the du test suite would follow the pattern `test-rel01.3` with a use case trace like `rel01.3-uc001-du`.\n\nSince du already imports `pkg/format` for size formatting and `pkg/sys` for file stat operations, I don't need to mock those dependencies. The main challenge is that du's output depends on actual block allocation from the filesystem, but since the differential test runs both the Go binary and gdu against the same files, they should produce matching results regardless of how blocks are allocated.\n\nLooking at the release constraints, I need to focus on the earliest incomplete release. Release 01.3 has the du command implemented but lacks tests, making it the priority. I should propose writing the test suite for du.\n\nFor the title, I'll use the grouped requirement format: \"prd009-du R1, R2, R4 -- cmd/du differential tests\". However, I'm noticing the test file size constraint of 250 lines is tighter than the existing test files in the codebase, so I'll need to be selective about which test cases to include.\n\nLooking at the scope, I can fit the essential pieces: TestMain setup, default behavior tests, the key flags like -s, -h, -b, and -c, basic error handling, and helper functions. That should come to around 160 lines, leaving room for adjustments.\n\nThe tricky part is that du's output depends on actual filesystem block allocation, but since this is differential testing comparing the Go implementation against gdu on the same files, both should produce consistent results as long as the test creates identical file structures for each invocation.\n\nI need to be careful about du's default behavior of walking the current directory—the test needs to set WorkDir to a controlled temporary directory so both binaries operate on the same known structure. Creating a temp directory with a specific structure, running both implementations against it, and comparing outputs should work fine since they're reading from the same filesystem.\n\nNow I'm looking at the flag support in the implementation. The code defines flags like `apparent-size`, `total`, `all`, `max-depth`, and `summarize`, but Go's flag package treats these as single-dash flags by default, not GNU-style double-dash flags. This could be a mismatch with how du is typically invoked, though that's more of an implementation concern than a test design issue—the differential tests should just use whatever flags the implementation actually supports and verify both binaries agree on the results.\n\nActually, Go's flag package does accept both single and double-dash syntax for registered flags, so `--apparent-size` should work fine. Let me move forward with writing out the task description, and I'll use `gdu` as the reference binary name since that's the Homebrew convention for GNU utilities on macOS.\n\nNow I'm laying out the full task structure with the required reading materials, the test file to create, and the specific requirements for implementing the differential test harness that will compare the Go du implementation against the GNU reference binary. I need to cover the remaining flags like human-readable output, apparent size in bytes, grand totals, and all files option, with each getting its own test case against the reference binary. Then I'm laying out the design decisions, starting with using the same testing utilities and patterns from the existing wc tests.\n\nEach test will set up a temporary directory to ensure both binaries operate on identical filesystem state and produce matching block counts. I'm also planning to add helper functions for creating files and directories with known sizes to make the block allocations predictable.\n\nThe test file needs to cover at least eight different scenarios including default behavior, various flag combinations like -s, -h, -b, -c, -a, and error handling for nonexistent paths. The file header will include the copyright notice, SPDX license identifier, package declaration, and comments referencing the relevant PRD and test documentation.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":79917,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":79917},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28f2da05-f954-4c15-bec7-cf50ae8af60a","uuid":"77f85465-434c-4223-a74e-361f1ef96000"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01T5HFc5WRe9Cq1PnbHTw3mL","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"## Project State Summary\n\n**Problem**: Classic Unix utilities lack formal specs. This project derives specs from C source, generates Go implementations via AI, and verifies parity through differential testing against GNU reference binaries.\n\n**Architecture**: `cmd/` (per-utility Go binaries), `pkg/testutils/` (differential test harness), `pkg/sys/` (syscall abstractions), `pkg/format/` (output formatting), `magefiles/` (build targets), `docs/` (specs/PRDs).\n\n**Current implementation** (from source_code — trusted over prose):\n- **Complete with tests**: `pkg/testutils`, `pkg/sys`, `pkg/format`, `cmd/wc`, `cmd/cat`, `cmd/sponge`, `cmd/version`\n- **Implementation exists, tests missing**: `cmd/du` — `main.go` exists (234 lines, covers prd009-du R1-R5) but no `du_test.go`\n\n**Releases in scope** (00.0–01.3): All code exists. The only gap is `cmd/du/du_test.go` in release 01.3.\n\n## Priority Analysis\n\nRelease 01.3 (`rel01.3-uc001-du`) is the earliest incomplete release. The du implementation is done but lacks differential tests. All dependencies are satisfied (pkg/testutils, pkg/sys, pkg/format all exist with tests).\n\n```yaml\n- index: 0\n  title: \"prd009-du R1, R2, R4 -- cmd/du differential tests\"\n  dependency: -1\n  description: |\n    deliverable_type: code\n\n    required_reading:\n      - cmd/du/main.go (implementation under test -- review all flags and output format)\n      - pkg/testutils/difftest.go (DiffTest struct and RunDiffTests harness API)\n      - cmd/wc/wc_test.go (canonical differential test pattern to follow)\n      - cmd/cat/cat_test.go (additional example of differential test structure)\n      - docs/specs/product-requirements/prd009-du.yaml (PRD requirements for du)\n\n    files:\n      - path: cmd/du/du_test.go\n        action: create\n        note: Differential tests comparing Go du binary against GNU gdu reference\n\n    requirements:\n      - id: R1\n        text: |\n          Implement TestMain that locates the GNU reference binary \"gdu\" via\n          exec.LookPath, builds the Go du binary to a temp directory with\n          \"go build -o <path> .\", and cleans up after tests complete. Skip all\n          tests (exit 0) when gdu is not found. Follow the exact TestMain pattern\n          from cmd/wc/wc_test.go.\n      - id: R2\n        text: |\n          Test default traversal and size reporting (prd009-du R1.1, R1.3, R1.5).\n          Create controlled directory trees with t.TempDir() containing known files\n          and subdirectories. Use testutils.RunDiffTests with WorkDir set to the\n          controlled directory. Cover: default (no flags) single directory, nested\n          subdirectories, and empty directory.\n      - id: R3\n        text: |\n          Test flag behaviors (prd009-du R2.1, R2.2, R2.7, R2.8). Cover: -s\n          (summarize), -h (human readable), -b (apparent size in bytes), -c (grand\n          total), and -a (all files). Each flag gets at least one DiffTest case\n          with a controlled directory tree.\n      - id: R4\n        text: |\n          Test error handling (prd009-du R4.2). Cover: nonexistent path argument\n          produces stderr and exit code 1. Use testutils.RunDiffTests to confirm\n          the Go binary matches gdu error behavior.\n\n    design_decisions:\n      - id: D1\n        text: |\n          Use testutils.RunDiffTests for all test cases. The reference binary is\n          \"gdu\" (GNU du from Homebrew). Follow the TestMain, writeFile, and helper\n          patterns from cmd/wc/wc_test.go exactly.\n      - id: D2\n        text: |\n          Every test function creates a controlled directory tree via t.TempDir()\n          and sets WorkDir on each DiffTest to that directory. This ensures both\n          binaries see identical filesystem state and produce identical block counts.\n      - id: D3\n        text: |\n          Add a writeFile helper (same signature as cmd/wc/wc_test.go) and a\n          mkdirAll helper that creates subdirectories within the test temp dir.\n          Use known file content sizes (e.g., 4096 bytes) to produce predictable\n          block allocations.\n\n    acceptance_criteria:\n      - id: AC1\n        text: cmd/du/du_test.go compiles with \"go build ./cmd/du/\"\n      - id: AC2\n        text: \"go test ./cmd/du/ passes when gdu is available on PATH\"\n      - id: AC3\n        text: |\n          Test file contains at least 8 DiffTest cases covering: default traversal,\n          -s, -h, -b, -c, -a flags, and nonexistent path error\n      - id: AC4\n        text: |\n          File header includes copyright, SPDX license, package main declaration,\n          Implements comment referencing prd009-du, and Traces comment referencing\n          test-rel01.3 and rel01.3-uc001-du\n```"}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":79917,"cache_read_input_tokens":19292,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":79917},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"28f2da05-f954-4c15-bec7-cf50ae8af60a","uuid":"5971e0b7-f87c-4b77-9f56-366d4c831d8e"}
